---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import PageHeader from '../components/PageHeader.astro';
import Icon from '../components/Icon.astro';
import { skills } from '../data/site';

const practices = [
  {
    title: 'Security by default',
    text: 'MFA, session management, audit logging, and role-based access control designed in from day one, not bolted on.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Architecture that scales',
    text: 'Multi-portal systems with clear role boundaries, a single source of truth, and shared components that hold up as the product grows.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Automated delivery',
    text: 'CI/CD pipelines that make deployments faster and safer, with checks that stop problems before they reach users.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Domain fluency',
    text: 'Comfortable in regulated domains: CCM/RPM programs, Medicare billing with CPT and ICD-10, EHR workflows, and union operations.',
  },
];
---

<Base
  title="Skills"
  description="Technical skills of Sām Kazemi: React, JavaScript, TypeScript, Python, AWS, CI/CD, HIPAA, Medicare billing, and AI-assisted engineering with Claude Code and Codex."
>
  <PageHeader
    eyebrow="Skills"
    title="The tools behind the work"
    lead="Full-stack JavaScript and React, AWS infrastructure, and deep familiarity with regulated healthcare."
  />

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="grid grid--2">
        {
          skills.map((s, i) => (
            <div class="card skill reveal" style={`--delay: ${(i % 2) * 0.08}s`}>
              <div class="skill__head">
                <span class="icon-badge">
                  <Icon name={s.icon} />
                </span>
                <h2>{s.group}</h2>
              </div>
              <ul class="skill__list">
                {s.items.map((item) => (
                  <li>{item}</li>
                ))}
              </ul>
            </div>
          ))
        }
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--alt ai">
    <div class="container ai__grid">
      <div class="ai__intro reveal">
        <p class="eyebrow">AI in his workflow</p>
        <h2>How he builds with AI</h2>
        <p class="lead">
          Sām uses AI every day, and treats it the way he treats any engineering tool: with
          discipline, the right configuration for the job, and a review of everything it produces.
        </p>
        <ul class="tag-list" aria-label="AI tools">
          <li class="tag">Claude Code</li>
          <li class="tag">Codex</li>
          <li class="tag">AI agents</li>
          <li class="tag">Prompt engineering</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <figure class="ai__quote card reveal" style="--delay: 0.08s">
        <svg class="ai__mark" viewBox="0 0 48 36" aria-hidden="true">
          <path d="M0 36V21C0 9 6 2 18 0l2 5c-6 2-9 6-9 12h8v19H0zm28 0V21C28 9 34 2 46 0l2 5c-6 2-9 6-9 12h8v19H28z" />
        </svg>
        <blockquote>
          <p>
            I use Claude Code and Codex every day as part of my engineering workflow, both to build
            new features and to maintain and audit production code. I’ve developed a structured
            approach to prompt engineering built to minimize hallucinations and AI-generated slop:
            every task gets the right context, the right constraints, and the right token budget. I
            use stronger models and deeper reasoning when I’m auditing code or reviewing a complex
            implementation, and lighter, more efficient configurations for straightforward work.
          </p>
          <p>
            I use these tools to organize repositories, write and run tests, produce implementation
            guides and sitemaps, maintain audit logs, and import or build custom skills that keep
            engineering guidelines consistent. I’ve also built AI agents that handle specific
            development tasks autonomously while operating inside clearly defined constraints.
          </p>
          <p>
            A major part of my workflow is quality control. I’m constantly looking for
            hallucinations, incorrect assumptions, duplicated or unnecessary code, and the other
            patterns that add up to slop. I review all AI-generated code and test every
            implementation myself, to catch both the obvious bugs and the “ghost bugs” that basic
            testing won’t surface. AI is an engineering tool and a collaborator, not an authority.
            The final responsibility for correctness stays with me.
          </p>
          <p class="ai__pivot">
            In the age of AI, a lot of companies are unsure how to hire. Some still screen with
            LeetCode, no-AI policies, and no-AI coding challenges. Those can show how an engineer
            thinks about code, but real work has moved on. Very little production code is written
            fully by hand anymore, even by senior engineers, so a no-AI coding challenge is a bit
            like asking a candidate to write in binary: it tests a skill nobody will ever use on the
            job.
          </p>
          <p>
            It also misses the bigger risk. Engineers hired through no-AI screens still use AI once
            they start, like every developer does, and without understanding how models behave, the
            patterns they fall into, and how to prompt them, the result is slop: code scattered with
            ghost bugs and low-quality patterns. What a team really needs is an engineer who knows
            how to use AI the right way. I believe that is now the most valuable skill in software,
            even more than writing code by hand. I stay current on the latest models, tools, and
            techniques and keep learning new methods every day, and that is the engineer I have
            made myself into.
          </p>
        </blockquote>
        <figcaption class="ai__cite">
          <span class="ai__name">Sām Kazemi</span>
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-head reveal">
        <p class="eyebrow">How he works</p>
        <h2>Engineering practices he brings to every team</h2>
      </div>
      <ol class="practices">
        {
          practices.map((p, i) => (
            <li class="reveal" style={`--delay: ${i * 0.06}s`}>
              <span class="practices__num">{String(i + 1).padStart(2, '0')}</span>
              <div>
                <h3>{p.title}</h3>
                <p class="muted">{p.text}</p>
              </div>
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>

</Base>

<style>
  .ai__grid {
    display: grid;
    gap: 40px;
  }

  @media (min-width: 1000px) {
    .ai__grid {
      grid-template-columns: 0.8fr 1.2fr;
      gap: 64px;
      align-items: start;
    }
    .ai__intro {
      position: sticky;
      top: 104px;
    }
  }

  .ai__intro .lead {
    margin-bottom: 24px;
  }

  .ai__quote {
    margin: 0;
    padding: clamp(28px, 4vw, 48px);
    border-top: 4px solid var(--gold);
  }

  .ai__mark {
    width: 44px;
    height: 33px;
    margin-bottom: 20px;
    fill: var(--gold-bright);
  }

  .ai__quote blockquote {
    margin: 0;
  }

  .ai__quote blockquote p {
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    font-size: 1.025rem;
    line-height: 1.75;
  }

  .ai__quote blockquote p:first-child {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: clamp(1.15rem, 1.8vw, 1.3rem);
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--ink);
  }

  /* The hiring argument starts here; a rule sets it apart from the workflow. */
  .ai__pivot {
    margin-top: 28px;
    padding-top: 28px;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--line);
  }

  .ai__cite {
    display: grid;
    gap: 2px;
    margin-top: 28px;
    padding-top: 20px;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--line);
  }

  .ai__name {
    font-weight: 800;
    color: var(--ink);
  }

  .skill__head {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 14px;
    margin-bottom: 22px;
  }

  .skill__head h2 {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: 1.25rem;
  }

  .skill__list {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 10px;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  .skill__list li {
    padding: 8px 14px;
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-radius: 10px;
    background: var(--bg);
    font-size: 0.925rem;
    font-weight: 500;
    color: var(--ink);
  }

  .practices {
    display: grid;
    gap: 16px;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  @media (min-width: 800px) {
    .practices {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr);
      gap: 24px 48px;
    }
  }

  .practices li {
    display: flex;
    gap: 20px;
    padding: 24px 0;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--line);
  }

  .practices__num {
    flex: none;
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: 1.6rem;
    font-style: italic;
    /* gold-strong, not gold-bright: bright gold on the light theme's ivory is under 2:1. */
    color: var(--gold-strong);
    line-height: 1.2;
  }

  .practices h3 {
    margin-bottom: 6px;
  }

  .practices p {
    margin: 0;
  }
</style>
